Three handlers take the id from the URL and never check it against the caller's account:

- `routes/accounts.js:6` PATCH `/accounts/:id` rewrites `display_name` and `billing_email` on any account.
- `routes/accounts.js:14` DELETE `/accounts/:id` soft-deletes any account.
- `routes/orders.js:14` PATCH `/orders/:id/cancel` cancels any order.

The session already has the value to scope by. `sessionUser` sets `req.user.accountId` (`auth.js:11`), and `POST /orders` already uses it (`routes/orders.js:9`). Put that value into each WHERE clause.

For orders, that is one added predicate:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

For accounts, the URL id has to be the caller's own account, so reject a mismatch first and bind the session value in SQL. The `String()` is there because the URL param is always a string and the column type isn't in the repo:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== String(req.user.accountId)) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== String(req.user.accountId)) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write("UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 RETURNING id",
    [req.user.accountId]);
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

I'd return 404 rather than 403 so a caller can't tell which account ids exist.

The `if (!row)` checks matter. `db.write` returns `rows[0]`, so an UPDATE that matches nothing gives `undefined`. `res.json(undefined)` then returns 200 with an empty body, so a blocked cross-account write looks like success. The DELETE currently returns 204 even when nothing matched.

Two things I noticed on these lines but did not fix:

- `auth.js:8` reads `req.cookies`, but `app.js` never mounts cookie-parser. In these files `req.cookies` is undefined, so every request gets a 401 before it reaches a route. There is no `package.json` here, so if cookie-parser is wired up somewhere else, ignore this. Until it is, the routes can't be exercised to test the fix.
- The accounts PATCH writes `NULL` into any field left out of the body, because an absent `req.body.display_name` becomes `undefined`, which node-pg sends as null. That is separate from scoping.

To test, have user A send PATCH and DELETE to account B and cancel an order owned by B. Each should return 404 and leave B's row unchanged. The repo has no tests.

A bigger option is Postgres row-level security, with the account id set inside `write()`, which already owns the transaction. That makes a missing predicate impossible, but it needs a schema change. The explicit predicates are the fix to make now.

