`withClient` (`db.js:11-16`) calls `client.release()` only if `fn(client)` resolves. If `fn` throws, the `await` rejects before `release()` runs, and the client stays checked out. The pool does not reclaim it. Each failed call permanently uses one of the 20 slots.

`pool.log` fits this. `idle=0` at every sample, and `waiting` climbs from 7 to 28 with nothing coming back. The `timeout exceeded when trying to connect` at 14:04:11 comes from `connectionTimeoutMillis: 2000` firing on those waiters.

The fix, which I have not applied, is to release in a `finally`:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

A larger pool would only delay saturation, since the leak keeps consuming slots. It would also add load on Postgres, so check `max_connections` on the server before going higher.

