Use Postgres triggers to write the audit rows, and have `write()` pass the acting user to the database. The audit row commits in the same transaction as the change, so neither commits without the other. Triggers also catch writes that bypass `write()`, which application-level logging would miss.

I read the five files in this directory. There are four write endpoints: `POST /orders`, `PATCH /orders/:id/cancel`, `PATCH /accounts/:id`, and `DELETE /accounts/:id`, which is a soft delete. I haven't run this sketch because there is no database here.

Schema and triggers:

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  changed_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT clock_timestamp(),
  table_name  text NOT NULL,
  row_id      text NOT NULL,
  action      text NOT NULL,            -- INSERT | UPDATE | DELETE
  actor_id    text,                     -- NULL when no actor was set
  db_role     text NOT NULL DEFAULT current_user,
  old_row     jsonb,
  new_row     jsonb
);

CREATE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO audit_log (table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, old_row, new_row)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    NULLIF(current_setting('app.user_id', true), ''),
    to_jsonb(OLD),
    to_jsonb(NEW)
  );
  RETURN NULL;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
CREATE TRIGGER orders_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON orders
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
```

Actor propagation in `db.js`:

```js
async function write(actor, text, params) {
  if (!actor || actor.id == null) throw new Error("write() requires an actor");
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.user_id', $1, true)", [String(actor.id)]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Each caller passes `req.user`, which `sessionUser` already sets. Background jobs pass an explicit identity such as `{ id: "system:billing-job" }`.

Notes on the design:
- `set_config(..., true)` is `SET LOCAL`. The actor lasts only for that transaction. A plain `SET` would leak to the next request on the pooled connection.
- A write that skips `write()` appears with a NULL `actor_id` and the database role in `db_role`. The gap is visible rather than silent.
- The app role must not own `accounts` or `orders`, since an owner can disable triggers. Revoke `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, and `TRUNCATE` on `audit_log` from it.
- I'd snapshot full rows. Storage is cheap next to reconstructing history from partial diffs.
- I didn't see a migrations directory here. The DDL belongs in whatever runs your schema changes.

