Orders:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

Accounts: the target is always the caller's own account, so reject any URL id that isn't theirs and key the query on `req.user.accountId`. Compare as strings, because `req.params.id` is a string and the pg driver may return `accountId` as a number.

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (String(req.user.accountId) !== req.params.id) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.user.accountId]
  );
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (String(req.user.accountId) !== req.params.id) return res.status(404).end();
  await db.write("UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 RETURNING id",
    [req.user.accountId]);
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

I return 404 rather than 403 for another account's id so the response doesn't reveal which ids exist. I haven't run any of this, since there is no database here.

Two things need a decision or a check:

- Any user on an account can now change billing details and delete the account. The schema has no role column, so if only an owner should do that, you need a role column and a check. I haven't designed that.
- Nothing in these files parses cookies. `app.js` never loads cookie-parser, so `req.cookies` is undefined and `sessionUser` returns 401 on every request as written. If cookie parsing is set up outside these files, ignore this. Otherwise the fix can't be exercised until it's added.

Add one middleware to `app.js`, placed before `app.use(sessionUser)` on line 11. That way 401s get logged too. It writes one JSON line per response when the response finishes:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.path,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

`req.user` is read at `finish`, so it is set for authenticated requests and absent for 401s. Don't log request bodies, since they carry `billing_email` and `display_name`. Don't log cookies or `req.user.email`.

The gap is failed requests. A line on `finish` never fires when a handler throws without responding, which is what happens when `write()` rejects (see the previous walkthrough). Add an error handler after the route mounts (`app.js:14`) to log those and return a 500:

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);
  res.status(500).end();
});
```

This works on Express 5, which forwards rejected async handlers to `next(err)`. On Express 4, it doesn't. Each async handler then needs a `try`/`catch` that calls `next(err)`, or a wrapper that does it. The repo has no `package.json`, so I can't tell which version you run.

Decision for you: plain `console.log` as above, or `pino-http`. `pino-http` gives you redaction and request IDs in exchange for a dependency.

